            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This breaks several abstraction boundaries as written in bad ways.  HTTP should have no knowledge of Redfish.  We shouldn't have to duplicate this logic between.  There's plenty of memory usage cleanups we need to do that I suspect are why you wrote this patch.  Lets get those landed, and see if you still have issues.  If you do, there's likely fixes."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "What I suspect we need instead is some level of checking for available system memory before we accept a payload."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Myung Bae",
                        "email": "myungbae@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "baemyung"
                    },
                    "message": "The example of parallel-or-continuous code update requests is used to illustrate the quicker consumption which easily leads into the bmcweb coredump.\n\nI would tend to agree to check available memory and compare with the payload size esp if payload size is bigger (e.g. code update payload) like\n```\n   if ( payload size > SOME-SIZE)\n   {\n         check available memory \n         if it is not enough ==> REJECT\n    }\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> The example of parallel-or-continuous code update requests is used to illustrate the quicker consumption which easily leads into the bmcweb coredump.\n\nYes, I don't need examples.  It's pretty trivial to reproduce with an authenticated user.  If you intentionally try to run the system out of memory, that's been a bug ever since we allowed people to set their payload size.\n\nYes, the pseudocode you've provided is the route i would go.  Remember that we check the payload size in a bunch of places, so please make sure they're not duplicated.  Do the check where we set the payload size in the max config."
                }
            ],
